<p>A philosopher who has written several books has a question about the publication of translations.<span id="more-56795"></span></p>
<div id="attachment_56796" style="width: 800px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-56796" class=" wp-image-56796" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations.png" alt="" width="790" height="600" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations.png 1819w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations-300x228.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations-1024x777.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations-768x583.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations-1536x1166.png 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/paolozzi-eduoardo-metafisikal-translations-400x304.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 790px) 100vw, 790px" /><p id="caption-attachment-56796" class="wp-caption-text">[Plate from &#8220;Metafisikal Translations&#8221; (detail) by Eduardo Paolozzi]</p></div>Often, translations of books are published by a publisher different from the one that put out the original book. As part of the agreement between the publishers, the new publisher pays a licensing fee to the original publisher for the rights to publish a translation in a specified language in a specified territory. Whether it makes economic sense for a publisher to put out a translation may depend on what the licensing fee is.</p>
<p>In this case, the author&#8217;s book was originally published by a well-known and well-regarded press. The process of publishing the translation has been underway, but the author was recently told by the person managing this process that the original publisher &#8220;has raised the licensing fee for this book to $3,000&#8221; and that &#8220;this amount presents a considerable strain on the [translating] publisher’s resources.&#8221;</p>
<p>The author is curious as to whether $3000 is a common licensing fee for academic books, and what goes into the determination of that fee. Another question is whether an author&#8217;s intervention in the process&#8212;say, by asking to lower the licensing fee&#8212;is appropriate or likely to be effective. Authors who have been in this position, as well as editors and others who work at publishers, are especially encouraged to comment. Thank you.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/03/05/licensing-fees-for-translations/">Licensing Fees for Translations</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>